[
{ch:11,q:"What is the empirical formula of a compound?",choices:["The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms of each element present in the compound","The actual number of atoms of each element present in one molecule","The arrangement of atoms shown using wedge and dash bonds","A shorthand formula that omits carbon and hydrogen symbols"],explain:"The empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms present, obtainable from percentage composition data such as combustion analysis."},
{ch:11,q:"How is the molecular formula related to the empirical formula?",choices:["The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple, n, of the empirical formula, where M = (molar mass of empirical formula) &times; n","The molecular formula is always identical to the empirical formula for every compound","The molecular formula is the empirical formula divided by the number of carbon atoms","The molecular formula shows only the ratio of hydrogen to oxygen atoms"],explain:"M = (molar mass of empirical formula) &times; n, so the molecular formula is the empirical formula multiplied by the whole number n = M &divide; (empirical mass)."},
{ch:11,q:"A compound has empirical formula CH<sub>2</sub>O (empirical mass 30 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) and molar mass M = 180 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What is its molecular formula?",choices:["C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>","C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>3</sub>","C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>5</sub>","C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O<sub>4</sub>"],explain:"n = 180 / 30 = 6, so the molecular formula is (CH<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub> = C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>, consistent with glucose."},
{ch:11,q:"What does a full structural (displayed) formula show?",choices:["Every bond and every atom in the molecule explicitly, using conventional 60&deg;, 90&deg; or 180&deg; angles for clarity","Only the ratio of atoms present, with no indication of bonding","Bonds only to non-hydrogen atoms, omitting carbon and hydrogen symbols","The three-dimensional arrangement of atoms using wedge and dash bonds"],explain:"A full structural formula shows every bond and atom explicitly; bond angles of 60&deg;, 90&deg; and 180&deg; are conventionally used on a flat page even though they are not the true geometry."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the key feature of a condensed structural formula?",choices:["It omits bonds that can be assumed while still containing enough information that only one structure is consistent with it","It shows every single bond explicitly, with no atoms omitted","It can represent more than one possible structure equally well","It omits the symbols for carbon and hydrogen entirely"],explain:"A condensed structural formula groups atoms together and omits assumed bonds, but retains the minimum information needed so that only one structure is consistent with it."},
{ch:11,q:"What bond angle is associated with the tetrahedral arrangement around a carbon such as in methanol, CH<sub>3</sub>OH?",choices:["109.5&deg;","120&deg;","90&deg;","180&deg;"],explain:"Four single bonds around a carbon give a tetrahedral arrangement with bond angle 109.5&deg;."},
{ch:11,q:"What bond angle is associated with the trigonal planar local arrangement around each carbon of ethene, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>?",choices:["120&deg;","109.5&deg;","90&deg;","60&deg;"],explain:"A double bond gives a trigonal planar local arrangement with bond angle 120&deg;, as around each carbon of ethene."},
{ch:11,q:"In a skeletal formula, what does a vertex with exactly two lines drawn to it (and no other element symbol) represent?",choices:["A CH<sub>2</sub> group, since each carbon has four bonds in total and two are drawn to non-hydrogen atoms","A CH<sub>3</sub> group","A CH group","A carbon with no attached hydrogens"],explain:"Since every carbon has four bonds, the number of hydrogens equals four minus the number of bonds drawn to non-hydrogen atoms; two drawn bonds leaves two hydrogens, a CH<sub>2</sub> group."},
{ch:11,q:"What is catenation?",choices:["The ability of an element's atoms to bond to one another, forming chains and rings","The ability of a compound to rotate plane-polarized light","The process of forming a peptide bond between amino acids","The tendency of alkanes to combust completely in oxygen"],explain:"Catenation is the ability of an element's atoms to bond to one another, forming chains and rings; carbon shows this to an exceptional degree."},
{ch:11,q:"What is a functional group?",choices:["An atom, or group of atoms, in an organic molecule responsible for its characteristic physical properties and chemical reactivity","The longest continuous carbon chain in a molecule","Any carbon atom bonded to exactly four different groups","The empirical formula of a homologous series"],explain:"Functional groups are atoms or groups of atoms responsible for a compound's physical properties and chemical reactivity; compounds sharing one belong to the same class."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the general formula of an alkane?",choices:["C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n&minus;2</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH"],explain:"Alkanes, containing only C&minus;C and C&minus;H bonds, follow the general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the general formula of an alkene?",choices:["C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n&minus;2</sub>","C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>X"],explain:"Alkenes, containing one C=C, follow the general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>."},
{ch:11,q:"Which IUPAC suffix corresponds to an alcohol?",choices:["-anol","-anoic acid","-anal","-anone"],explain:"The alcohol class, with the hydroxyl functional group &minus;OH, takes the suffix -anol."},
{ch:11,q:"Which IUPAC suffix corresponds to a carboxylic acid?",choices:["-anoic acid","-anol","-anoate","-anamide"],explain:"The carboxylic acid class, with the carboxyl functional group &minus;COOH, takes the suffix -anoic acid."},
{ch:11,q:"How are halogen substituents (fluoro, chloro, bromo, iodo) named in a halogenoalkane?",choices:["Always as prefixes, since the halogen has simply taken the position of a hydrogen atom","Always as suffixes, replacing the -ane ending","Only as suffixes when there is more than one halogen present","As a separate word written after the rest of the name, never joined to it"],explain:"Halogens are treated as substituents, having taken the position of a hydrogen atom, and so are always named with prefixes rather than a suffix."},
{ch:11,q:"What distinguishes an unsaturated compound from a saturated compound?",choices:["An unsaturated compound contains one or more double or triple bonds; a saturated compound contains only single bonds","An unsaturated compound contains only single bonds; a saturated compound contains double or triple bonds","Unsaturated compounds always contain nitrogen; saturated compounds never do","Unsaturated compounds cannot undergo any chemical reactions"],explain:"Saturated compounds contain only single bonds; unsaturated compounds contain one or more double or triple bonds."},
{ch:11,q:"Which type of reaction specifically requires an unsaturated site (a double or triple bond) in the molecule?",choices:["Addition reactions","Substitution reactions","Condensation reactions only","All organic reactions require an unsaturated site"],explain:"Addition reactions require an unsaturated site, while substitution reactions can occur on saturated carbon skeletons."},
{ch:11,q:"What functional group defines an ester, and what is its general formula?",choices:["&minus;COO&minus; (R&minus;COO&minus;R&prime;), formed when the alkyl group of an alcohol replaces the acidic H of a carboxylic acid","&minus;CONH<sub>2</sub>, formed from a carboxylic acid and ammonia","&minus;OH, formed by addition of water to an alkene","R&minus;O&minus;R&prime;, formed from two alcohol molecules joining directly"],explain:"Esters form when the alkyl group of an alcohol replaces the acidic hydrogen of a carboxylic acid, R&minus;COOH + R&prime;OH &rarr; R&minus;COO&minus;R&prime; + H<sub>2</sub>O."},
{ch:11,q:"What is a reaction pathway in organic synthesis?",choices:["A sequence of reactions in which the product of one step becomes the reactant of the next, converting a starting material into a target compound","A single reaction that converts a starting material directly into a target compound with no intermediates","The mechanism by which electrons move during a single reaction step","A diagram showing only the intermolecular forces present in a reaction mixture"],explain:"A reaction pathway organizes several reactions in sequence, each step typically a functional group interconversion, so the product of one step becomes the reactant of the next."},
{ch:11,q:"Ethene can be converted into ethanoic acid via an ethanol intermediate. What are the two steps, in order?",choices:["Addition of water to ethene (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> catalyst) to form ethanol, then oxidation of ethanol to ethanoic acid","Oxidation of ethene to ethanol, then addition of water to form ethanoic acid","Addition of water to ethene to form ethanoic acid directly, with no intermediate","Reduction of ethene to ethanol, then reduction of ethanol to ethanoic acid"],explain:"CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH (addition, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> catalyst), then CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH (oxidation)."},
{ch:11,q:"What two functional groups does every amino acid contain?",choices:["An amine group (&minus;NH<sub>2</sub>) and a carboxylic acid group (&minus;COOH)","A hydroxyl group (&minus;OH) and an aldehyde group (&minus;CHO)","Two carboxylic acid groups (&minus;COOH)","An ester group and an amine group"],explain:"Amino acids each contain an amine group and a carboxylic acid group, which react together in a condensation reaction to form a peptide bond."},
{ch:11,q:"How does a peptide bond form between two amino acids?",choices:["A condensation reaction between the &minus;COOH of one amino acid and the &minus;NH<sub>2</sub> of another, eliminating a molecule of water","An addition reaction across the double bond of one amino acid","A reaction that releases ammonia, NH<sub>3</sub>, rather than water","A reaction between two &minus;COOH groups, releasing carbon dioxide"],explain:"A peptide bond is a substituted amide link formed by condensation between the acid group of one amino acid and the amino group of another, releasing H<sub>2</sub>O."},
{ch:11,q:"What defines a homologous series?",choices:["A family of compounds in which successive members differ by a common structural unit (typically CH<sub>2</sub>) and which share a single general formula","A group of compounds that all have exactly the same molecular formula","A family of compounds that differ only in the position of a functional group","A set of isomers with identical physical properties"],explain:"A homologous series is a family of compounds in which successive members differ by a common structural unit, typically CH<sub>2</sub>, described by a single general formula."},
{ch:11,q:"Why does boiling point increase with increasing carbon number within a homologous series such as the alkanes?",choices:["A longer chain increases the instantaneous and induced dipoles between molecules, strengthening London (dispersion) forces","A longer chain increases the permanent dipole moment of each molecule","A longer chain allows more hydrogen bonds to form between molecules","A longer chain reduces the molar mass of the compound"],explain:"A longer chain increases both the instantaneous and induced dipoles between molecules, strengthening London dispersion forces and raising boiling point."},
{ch:11,q:"Arrange the following classes from most volatile to least volatile, for compounds of similar chain length: alkane, alcohol, carboxylic acid, aldehyde.",choices:["Alkane &gt; aldehyde &gt; alcohol &gt; carboxylic acid","Carboxylic acid &gt; alcohol &gt; aldehyde &gt; alkane","Alcohol &gt; alkane &gt; carboxylic acid &gt; aldehyde","Aldehyde &gt; carboxylic acid &gt; alkane &gt; alcohol"],explain:"From most to least volatile: alkane &gt; halogenoalkane &gt; aldehyde &gt; ketone &gt; alcohol &gt; carboxylic acid, matching increasing strength of intermolecular force."},
{ch:11,q:"Comparing compounds with the same number of carbons, which order of boiling point is correct?",choices:["Alkane &lt; chloroalkane &lt; alcohol","Alcohol &lt; chloroalkane &lt; alkane","Chloroalkane &lt; alkane &lt; alcohol","Alcohol &lt; alkane &lt; chloroalkane"],explain:"Alkane &lt; chloroalkane &lt; alcohol in boiling point, consistent with London forces only &lt; dipole&ndash;dipole &lt; hydrogen bonding."},
{ch:11,q:"Why does branching of a carbon chain lower the boiling point relative to its straight-chain isomer?",choices:["Branching reduces the surface contact between neighbouring molecules, weakening London dispersion forces","Branching increases the permanent dipole moment of the molecule","Branching introduces hydrogen bonding that was not present in the straight chain","Branching increases the molar mass of the molecule"],explain:"Branching reduces surface contact between neighbouring molecules, weakening London dispersion forces and lowering boiling point, like branched logs that cannot stack as closely."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is methanol a liquid at room temperature while methane and chloromethane are both gases?",choices:["Hydrogen bonding between methanol molecules is strong enough to raise its boiling point above room temperature, unlike the weaker forces in methane and chloromethane","Methanol has a much higher molar mass than methane or chloromethane","Methanol is ionic, while methane and chloromethane are covalent","Methane and chloromethane both contain stronger dipole-dipole forces than methanol"],explain:"Hydrogen bonding in alcohols is strong enough that even methanol is a liquid at room temperature, while methane and chloromethane, lacking this force, are gases."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the IUPAC stem for a four-carbon chain?",choices:["but-","prop-","pent-","meth-"],explain:"The stem for a four-carbon chain is but-, as in butane, C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>."},
{ch:11,q:"When numbering a carbon chain to name a compound, which end should be chosen?",choices:["Whichever end gives the smallest possible locant for the principal functional group","Always the end nearest to the largest substituent by mass","Always the left-hand end as the molecule is drawn","Whichever end gives the largest possible locant for the principal functional group"],explain:"The chain is numbered from whichever end gives the smallest possible locant for the functional group."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the IUPAC name of CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub>?",choices:["butan-2-ol","butan-3-ol","butan-1-ol","2-methylpropan-1-ol"],explain:"The four-carbon chain has &minus;OH on the carbon that is position 2 when numbered from the end nearer to it, giving the lowest locant: butan-2-ol."},
{ch:11,q:"Substituents in an IUPAC name are listed in which order?",choices:["Alphabetically, each with its own locant number","In order of increasing locant number, regardless of alphabetical order","In order of decreasing molar mass","In the order they would be added during synthesis"],explain:"Substituents are numbered to give the lowest overall locants and listed alphabetically, each with its own locant."},
{ch:11,q:"In the amino acid alanine, CH<sub>3</sub>CH(NH<sub>2</sub>)COOH, why is the compound named 2-aminopropanoic acid rather than using an amine suffix?",choices:["The carboxylic acid group has higher naming priority and takes the suffix, so the amino group becomes a prefix instead","Amine groups can never be named as a suffix under any circumstances","Carboxylic acid groups are always named as prefixes when an amine is present","The molecule contains no carboxylic acid group, only an amine group"],explain:"When a higher-priority group such as &minus;COOH is also present, amino becomes a prefix rather than the suffix, as in 2-aminopropanoic acid (alanine)."},
{ch:11,q:"Ethanoic acid reacts with propan-1-ol to form an ester. What is this ester's IUPAC name?",choices:["propyl ethanoate","ethyl propanoate","propyl propanoate","ethyl ethanoate"],explain:"The stem comes from the parent acid (ethanoic acid, giving ethanoate) and the alcohol's alkyl group (propyl from propan-1-ol) is the prefix: propyl ethanoate."},
{ch:11,q:"What are the three possible parts of a complete IUPAC name, and what does each represent?",choices:["Prefix (substituent position/number/name), stem (longest chain length), and suffix (class, from the principal functional group)","Prefix (functional group), stem (number of hydrogens), and suffix (molecular formula)","Only a stem and a suffix; organic names never include a prefix","Prefix (molar mass), stem (boiling point), and suffix (density)"],explain:"An IUPAC name has up to three parts: a prefix (substituents), a stem (longest chain), and a suffix (principal functional group)."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the defining feature of structural isomers?",choices:["The same molecular formula, but different connectivity (different arrangement of bonds between atoms)","The same connectivity, but a different spatial arrangement in three dimensions","Different molecular formulas but identical physical properties","The same empirical formula, but different numbers of chiral centres"],explain:"Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in connectivity, and therefore in physical and chemical properties."},
{ch:11,q:"What type of structural isomerism is shown by but-1-ene and but-2-ene, both C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>?",choices:["Positional isomerism, where the C=C double bond occupies a different position along the same chain length","Chain isomerism, where the carbon skeleton itself is branched differently","Functional group isomerism, where the two belong to different classes of compound","Optical isomerism, arising from a chiral carbon"],explain:"Alkenes show positional isomerism, where the C=C can occupy different positions along the same chain length, as in but-1-ene and but-2-ene."},
{ch:11,q:"C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O can represent two different functional group isomers. What are they?",choices:["Ethanol (an alcohol) and methoxymethane (an ether)","Ethanal (an aldehyde) and ethanoic acid (a carboxylic acid)","Ethene (an alkene) and ethane (an alkane)","Propanone (a ketone) and propan-1-ol (an alcohol)"],explain:"C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O describes both ethanol (an alcohol) and methoxymethane (an ether), structural isomers from entirely different classes."},
{ch:11,q:"C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O can represent two different functional group isomers. What are they?",choices:["Propanal (an aldehyde) and propanone (a ketone)","Propan-1-ol (an alcohol) and propan-2-ol (another alcohol)","Propanoic acid (a carboxylic acid) and methyl ethanoate (an ester)","Propene (an alkene) and propyne (an alkyne)"],explain:"C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O describes both propanal (an aldehyde) and propanone (a ketone), functional group isomers of each other."},
{ch:11,q:"How many structural isomers exist for monosubstituted benzene, and why?",choices:["None; all six ring positions are chemically equivalent, so only one structure is possible","Three, corresponding to 1,2-, 1,3-, and 1,4- substitution patterns","Six, one for each possible ring position","Two, an ortho and a para form"],explain:"No structural isomers exist for monosubstituted benzene, since all six ring positions are equivalent."},
{ch:11,q:"How many structural isomers exist for a disubstituted benzene, such as dibromobenzene, and what are they called?",choices:["Three: 1,2- (adjacent), 1,3- (one position apart), and 1,4- (directly opposite)","Six, one for every possible pair of ring positions","One; disubstituted benzene has no structural isomers","Two: ortho and meta only"],explain:"Three structural isomers exist for a disubstituted benzene: 1,2-, 1,3-, and 1,4-, corresponding to the three distinct relative arrangements of the two substituents."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is the existence of only three structural isomers of disubstituted benzene considered evidence for benzene's structure?",choices:["It is consistent with a delocalized, symmetric ring, whereas a structure with fixed alternating single and double bonds would predict a larger number of isomers","It proves that benzene contains no carbon-carbon double bonds at all","It shows that benzene cannot undergo substitution reactions","It proves that every ring position in benzene carries a different partial charge"],explain:"That only three isomers are observed, rather than the larger number predicted by fixed alternating bonds, is itself evidence for benzene's delocalized, symmetric ring structure."},
{ch:11,q:"What defines a tertiary carbon bearing a functional group, such as in 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COH?",choices:["It is attached to the functional group and three alkyl groups, with no hydrogens directly on that carbon","It is attached to the functional group and at least two hydrogens","It is attached to the functional group, one hydrogen, and two alkyl groups","It is attached to four different alkyl groups and no functional group"],explain:"A tertiary carbon is attached to the functional group and three alkyl groups, with no hydrogens, as in 2-methylpropan-2-ol."},
{ch:11,q:"How is a secondary carbon, as in propan-2-ol, CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub>, classified?",choices:["Attached to the functional group, one hydrogen, and two alkyl groups","Attached to the functional group and at least two hydrogens","Attached to the functional group and three alkyl groups, with no hydrogens","Attached to no hydrogens and no alkyl groups"],explain:"A secondary carbon is attached to the functional group, one hydrogen, and two alkyl groups, as in propan-2-ol."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the key difference between structural isomers and stereoisomers?",choices:["Structural isomers differ in connectivity; stereoisomers have identical connectivity but differ in spatial, three-dimensional arrangement","Structural isomers differ in spatial arrangement; stereoisomers differ in connectivity","Structural isomers and stereoisomers are simply two names for the same phenomenon","Structural isomers always have different molecular formulas; stereoisomers never do"],explain:"Structural isomers differ in connectivity &mdash; which atoms are bonded to which. Stereoisomers have identical connectivity but differ in spatial arrangement."},
{ch:11,q:"How does stereoisomerism split into two further categories?",choices:["Conformational isomerism (interconverted by free rotation about single bonds) and configurational isomerism (a fixed difference requiring bonds to be broken to interconvert)","Chain isomerism and positional isomerism","Empirical isomerism and molecular isomerism","Enantiomer isomerism and diastereomer isomerism, with no other categories"],explain:"Stereoisomerism splits into conformational isomerism (interconverted by bond rotation) and configurational isomerism (requiring bonds to be broken to interconvert)."},
{ch:11,q:"What are the two conformers of cyclohexane, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>, and which is more stable?",choices:["The boat and chair forms, interconverting by ring-flipping; the chair form is more stable and predominant","The cis and trans forms, interconverting by ring-flipping; the cis form is more stable","The boat and chair forms; the boat form is more stable and predominant","The R and S forms, which cannot interconvert at all"],explain:"Cyclohexane's two conformers are the boat and chair forms, interconverting by ring-flipping through bond rotation; the chair form is more stable and predominant."},
{ch:11,q:"Why can conformational isomers not normally be isolated as separate compounds, unlike configurational isomers?",choices:["They interconvert spontaneously through rotation about single bonds, without any covalent bond needing to break","They are chemically identical to the starting material and never actually form","They can only be distinguished using a mass spectrometer, not isolated","They require breaking a covalent bond to interconvert, exactly like configurational isomers"],explain:"Conformational isomers interconvert spontaneously by bond rotation, with no bond breaking required, so they usually cannot be isolated as separate compounds."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is free rotation about a C=C double bond not possible?",choices:["Rotating one carbon relative to the other would twist the parallel p orbitals out of alignment and break the pi bond","The two carbons of a double bond are held together only by London dispersion forces","A double bond consists of two identical sigma bonds, neither of which can rotate","Double bonds are shorter than single bonds, which alone prevents any rotation"],explain:"A C=C double bond consists of a sigma bond and a pi bond; rotating one carbon relative to the other would twist the p orbitals out of alignment and break the pi bond."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the essential criterion for cis-trans isomerism to be possible at a C=C double bond?",choices:["Each carbon of the C=C must bear two different substituent groups","At least one carbon of the C=C must bear two identical substituent groups","The molecule must contain a chiral carbon elsewhere in the structure","The double bond must be located at the very end of the carbon chain"],explain:"Cis-trans isomerism requires each carbon of the C=C to bear two different groups; if either carbon bears two identical groups, only one arrangement is possible."},
{ch:11,q:"Why does CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl show no cis-trans isomerism?",choices:["One carbon of the double bond carries two identical hydrogen atoms, so only one arrangement is possible","Chlorine is too large an atom to participate in cis-trans isomerism","The molecule has no double bond at all","CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl actually does show cis-trans isomerism, with two distinct forms"],explain:"In CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl, one carbon carries two hydrogens (identical substituents), so no cis-trans isomerism occurs."},
{ch:11,q:"In cis-but-2-ene, CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>3</sub>, how are the two methyl groups arranged relative to the double bond?",choices:["On the same side of the C=C, with the two hydrogen atoms together on the other side","On opposite sides of the C=C","Both methyl groups are bonded to the same carbon atom","One methyl group is bonded to the double bond carbon, and one is not"],explain:"In cis-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups lie on the same side of the C=C, with the two H atoms together on the other side."},
{ch:11,q:"What reference plane is used to describe cis and trans substituents on a cyclic (ring) molecule?",choices:["The plane of the ring itself","A plane perpendicular to every C&minus;C bond in the ring","The plane defined by the four different groups on a chiral carbon","There is no reference plane; ring substituents cannot show cis-trans isomerism"],explain:"For a ring, the reference plane is the plane of the ring; substituents can lie on the same side (cis) or opposite sides (trans) of this plane."},
{ch:11,q:"In cyclic cis-trans isomerism, must the two substituted carbons be directly adjacent to each other?",choices:["No; only their relative position above or below the ring plane matters, not their adjacency","Yes, the substituted carbons must always be directly bonded to each other","Yes, and they must also be the only two carbons in the ring","No, but the ring must contain exactly three carbon atoms"],explain:"The substituted carbons on a ring need not be adjacent; only their relative position above or below the ring plane matters."},
{ch:11,q:"Maleic acid (cis-butenedioic acid) and fumaric acid (trans-butenedioic acid) are isomers of HOOC&minus;CH=CH&minus;COOH. Which statement about their biological roles is correct?",choices:["Fumaric acid (trans) is a Krebs cycle intermediate, while maleic acid (cis) inhibits certain amino-acid-interconverting enzymes","Maleic acid (cis) is a Krebs cycle intermediate, while fumaric acid (trans) inhibits those enzymes","Both isomers have identical biological effects, since cis and trans isomers never differ in reactivity","Neither isomer has any known biological role"],explain:"Fumaric acid (trans) is a Krebs cycle intermediate in aerobic respiration, while maleic acid (cis) inhibits enzymes that interconvert amino acids."},
{ch:11,q:"What makes a carbon atom chiral?",choices:["It is bonded to four different atoms or groups, arranged tetrahedrally","It is bonded to four identical groups, arranged tetrahedrally","It is part of a C=C double bond with two different substituents on each carbon","It is bonded to exactly three different groups and one hydrogen"],explain:"A carbon bonded to four different atoms or groups, arranged tetrahedrally, is chiral (asymmetric), giving rise to two enantiomers."},
{ch:11,q:"Why must the whole attached group, not just the directly bonded atom, be compared when identifying a chiral carbon?",choices:["Two different groups, such as &minus;CH<sub>3</sub> and &minus;C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>, both begin with a carbon atom but are still different groups overall","Only the directly bonded atom's mass needs to be compared; the rest of the group is irrelevant","Chiral carbons can only be bonded to atoms, never to groups of atoms","Comparing whole groups is only necessary for nitrogen-containing molecules"],explain:"The whole attached group must be compared: &minus;CH<sub>3</sub> is a different group from &minus;C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>, even though both begin with carbon."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the relationship between a pair of enantiomers?",choices:["They are non-superimposable mirror images of each other","They are superimposable mirror images of each other","They differ in molecular formula but not in connectivity","They have opposite configuration at only some of their chiral centres"],explain:"Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of each other, arising from the two possible configurations at a chiral carbon."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is a chiral molecule often compared to a human hand?",choices:["A chiral molecule has no internal plane of symmetry, just as the left and right hand are non-superimposable mirror images","Because chiral molecules always contain exactly five chiral carbons, like the five fingers of a hand","Because a hand has a plane of symmetry, exactly like a chiral molecule","Because chiral molecules can only be synthesized using biological catalysts"],explain:"A chiral molecule has no internal plane of symmetry, exactly as a hand is a non-superimposable mirror image of the other hand."},
{ch:11,q:"In butan-2-ol, CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, which four groups are attached to its chiral carbon?",choices:["&minus;OH, &minus;H, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, and &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>","&minus;OH, &minus;H, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, and &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>","&minus;OH, &minus;OH, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, and &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>","&minus;H, &minus;H, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, and &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>"],explain:"The chiral carbon of butan-2-ol is bonded to &minus;OH, &minus;H, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, and &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> &mdash; four different groups."},
{ch:11,q:"In 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid), CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)COOH, which four groups are attached to its chiral carbon?",choices:["&minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, &minus;OH, &minus;COOH, and &minus;H","&minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, &minus;COOH, and &minus;H","&minus;OH, &minus;OH, &minus;COOH, and &minus;H","&minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, &minus;OH, &minus;COOH, and &minus;COOH"],explain:"The chiral carbon of lactic acid is bonded to &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, &minus;OH, &minus;COOH and &minus;H &mdash; four different groups."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the maximum possible number of stereoisomers for a molecule with n chiral centres?",choices:["2<sup>n</sup>","n<sup>2</sup>","2n","n + 1"],explain:"A molecule with n chiral centres has a maximum of 2<sup>n</sup> possible stereoisomers, though some may be too strained to exist in practice."},
{ch:11,q:"How do enantiomers and diastereomers differ, for a molecule with two or more chiral centres?",choices:["Enantiomers have the opposite configuration at every chiral centre (complete mirror images); diastereomers have the opposite configuration at only some chiral centres","Enantiomers have the opposite configuration at only some chiral centres; diastereomers have the opposite configuration at every chiral centre","Enantiomers and diastereomers are identical in every physical and chemical property","Diastereomers are always mirror images of each other, exactly like enantiomers"],explain:"Enantiomers have opposite configuration at every chiral centre, so they are complete mirror images; diastereomers differ at only some chiral centres and are not mirror images."},
{ch:11,q:"Glucose and galactose are related as which type of isomer?",choices:["Diastereomers","Enantiomers","Chain isomers","Conformational isomers"],explain:"Common sugars such as glucose and galactose are diastereomers of each other, differing in configuration at only some of their chiral centres."},
{ch:11,q:"What does it mean for a substance to be optically active?",choices:["It rotates the plane of plane-polarized light passing through it","It absorbs infrared radiation at a characteristic wavenumber","It conducts electricity when dissolved in water","It always exists as a racemic mixture"],explain:"A substance is optically active if it rotates the plane of plane-polarized light passing through it."},
{ch:11,q:"How do the two enantiomers of a chiral compound, at equal concentration, affect plane-polarized light?",choices:["They rotate it by an equal angle but in opposite directions","They rotate it by an equal angle in the same direction","Only one enantiomer rotates plane-polarized light; the other has no effect on it","Neither enantiomer has any effect on plane-polarized light"],explain:"The two enantiomers of a chiral compound, at equal concentration, rotate plane-polarized light by an equal angle but in opposite directions."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is a racemic mixture optically inactive overall, even though each pure enantiomer within it is optically active alone?",choices:["The equal and opposite rotation caused by each enantiomer, present in equal concentration, exactly cancels","A racemic mixture contains no chiral molecules at all","Racemic mixtures contain only one of the two enantiomers","The two enantiomers in a racemic mixture chemically react with each other, destroying their chirality"],explain:"A racemic mixture contains equal concentrations of both enantiomers, and their equal and opposite rotation exactly cancels, giving no net rotation."},
{ch:11,q:"How do naturally occurring chiral molecules typically compare with those made by ordinary laboratory synthesis from non-chiral starting materials?",choices:["Naturally occurring chiral molecules usually exist as essentially one enantiomer, whereas laboratory synthesis typically produces a racemic (optically inactive) mixture","Naturally occurring chiral molecules are always racemic, while laboratory synthesis always gives one pure enantiomer","Both are always produced as a racemic mixture, with no exceptions","Neither natural nor synthetic sources ever produce optically active compounds"],explain:"Naturally occurring chiral molecules (sugars, amino acids) are usually optically active, existing as essentially one enantiomer, while ordinary laboratory synthesis typically gives a racemic mixture."},
{ch:11,q:"What do the labels (+) and (&minus;) describe, when applied to an enantiomer?",choices:["The direction a compound rotates plane-polarized light: (+) clockwise (dextrorotatory), (&minus;) anticlockwise (laevorotatory)","The electrical charge on the molecule in solution","Whether the compound is classified using the D-/L- system or the R-/S- system","Whether the compound is a diastereomer (+) or an enantiomer (&minus;)"],explain:"(+), or dextrorotatory, rotates plane-polarized light clockwise; (&minus;), or laevorotatory, rotates it anticlockwise."},
{ch:11,q:"In the thalidomide case, what made the tragedy especially difficult to prevent even with knowledge of which enantiomer was safe?",choices:["The two enantiomers were found to interconvert inside the body, so administering only the pure safe (R) enantiomer would not have prevented the teratogenic effect","Both enantiomers were equally safe, and the birth defects were caused by an unrelated impurity","Thalidomide has no chiral carbon, so enantiomers were never actually a factor","The S enantiomer was the effective, safe sedative, while the R enantiomer was teratogenic"],explain:"The two enantiomers of thalidomide interconvert inside the body, so even administering only the pure safe R enantiomer would not have prevented the teratogenic effect of the S enantiomer forming in vivo."},
{ch:11,q:"Why can two enantiomers of a compound react completely differently with another chiral substance, despite having identical reactivity towards achiral reagents?",choices:["A chiral environment, such as an enzyme active site, can accommodate one enantiomer's shape better than the other's, just as a right-hand glove fits only a right hand","One enantiomer is always more chemically reactive than the other towards every type of reagent","Chiral substances cannot react with any organic compound at all","Enantiomers differ in molecular formula, which changes their reactivity towards chiral reagents"],explain:"A chiral environment can accommodate one enantiomer's shape better than the other's, in the same way a right-hand glove fits a right hand but not a left hand."},
{ch:11,q:"What happens to a molecule in a mass spectrometer to form the molecular ion?",choices:["A high-energy electron beam knocks one electron out of the molecule, forming a positively charged ion of essentially unchanged mass","The molecule gains an extra electron, forming a negatively charged ion","The molecule absorbs a photon of infrared radiation, causing a bond to vibrate","The molecule loses a proton, forming a negatively charged ion"],explain:"X(g) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; X<sup>+</sup>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>: a high-energy electron beam knocks an electron out of the molecule, forming the molecular (parent) ion."},
{ch:11,q:"What does the m/z value of the molecular ion (parent ion) peak directly give?",choices:["The relative molecular mass of the compound","The number of distinct proton environments in the compound","The specific functional group present in the compound","The boiling point of the compound"],explain:"The molecular ion's m/z value, usually the peak at the highest m/z in the spectrum, gives the relative molecular mass of the compound directly."},
{ch:11,q:"Why are only positively charged fragments detected in a mass spectrometer?",choices:["The instrument's electric and magnetic fields have no effect on neutral fragments, so only charged ions are deflected and detected","Neutral fragments are destroyed instantly upon formation","Negatively charged fragments are always formed in greater abundance and mask the neutral ones","Only positively charged species have mass, so neutral fragments do not register at all"],explain:"Electric and magnetic fields inside the instrument have no effect on neutral fragments, so only positively charged fragments are detected."},
{ch:11,q:"When a bond in a molecular ion breaks to give two possible fragments, which one is preferentially observed as a cation?",choices:["Whichever fragment forms the more stable cation","Whichever fragment has the smaller mass, regardless of stability","Whichever fragment has the larger mass, regardless of stability","Both fragments are always observed with exactly equal intensity"],explain:"For each bond that breaks, the positive charge ends up on whichever fragment forms the more stable cation."},
{ch:11,q:"Ethanol, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH (M = 46), shows a peak at m/z = 45. What does this correspond to?",choices:["Loss of one hydrogen atom from the molecular ion (46 &minus; 1 = 45)","Loss of a methyl group, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, from the molecular ion","Loss of a hydroxyl group, &minus;OH, from the molecular ion","The complete fragmentation of the molecule into two equal halves"],explain:"A peak at m/z = 45 corresponds to loss of one H atom from the molecular ion (46 &minus; 1 = 45)."},
{ch:11,q:"Ethanol's mass spectrum shows a strong peak at m/z = 29 but essentially none at m/z = 17. Why?",choices:["Breaking the C&minus;O bond preferentially gives the more stable C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup> ion (losing &minus;OH) rather than OH<sup>+</sup> (losing &minus;C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)","Breaking the C&minus;C bond always gives exactly these two masses","m/z = 17 corresponds to the molecular ion itself, so it is not a fragment peak","Ethanol contains no oxygen atom, so no OH<sup>+</sup> ion could ever form"],explain:"Breaking the C&minus;O bond preferentially gives the more stable C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup> ion (m/z = 29, loss of &minus;OH) rather than the less stable OH<sup>+</sup> (m/z = 17)."},
{ch:11,q:"According to the common fragment-loss table, what neutral fragment corresponds to a mass loss of 17 from the molecular ion?",choices:["&minus;OH","&minus;CH<sub>3</sub>","H<sub>2</sub>O","&minus;COOH"],explain:"A mass loss of 17 corresponds to loss of &minus;OH, according to the common fragment-loss table."},
{ch:11,q:"According to the common fragment-loss table, what neutral fragment corresponds to a mass loss of 45 from the molecular ion?",choices:["&minus;COOH","&minus;OCH<sub>3</sub>","&minus;CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>","CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"A mass loss of 45 corresponds to loss of &minus;COOH, according to the common fragment-loss table."},
{ch:11,q:"A compound's molecular ion appears at m/z = 88. A fragment peak appears at m/z = 73. What mass was lost, and what fragment does this most likely correspond to?",choices:["15, corresponding to loss of &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>","17, corresponding to loss of &minus;OH","73, corresponding to loss of the entire molecule","28, corresponding to loss of CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub>"],explain:"88 &minus; 73 = 15, corresponding to loss of a methyl group, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, according to the common fragment-loss table."},
{ch:11,q:"A compound's molecular ion appears at m/z = 102. A fragment peak appears at m/z = 57. What mass was lost, and what fragment does this most likely correspond to?",choices:["45, corresponding to loss of &minus;COOH","57, corresponding to loss of &minus;OH","102, corresponding to loss of the whole molecule","15, corresponding to loss of &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>"],explain:"102 &minus; 57 = 45, corresponding to loss of &minus;COOH, according to the common fragment-loss table."},
{ch:11,q:"An unknown compound has empirical formula CH<sub>2</sub>O (empirical mass 30 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) and a molecular ion at m/z = 60. What is its molecular formula?",choices:["C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O<sub>2</sub>","CH<sub>2</sub>O","C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>3</sub>","C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O"],explain:"60 / 30 = 2, so the molecular formula is C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, consistent with ethanoic acid."},
{ch:11,q:"An unknown compound has molecular formula C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>, giving a molecular ion at m/z = 84. What does the comparison with the saturated acyclic formula C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>14</sub> (hexane) show?",choices:["The compound must contain either one C=C double bond or one ring, since it has two fewer hydrogens than the saturated acyclic structure","The compound must be fully saturated and acyclic, identical in structure to hexane","The compound must contain exactly two rings","The compound cannot contain any carbon-carbon multiple bonds under any circumstances"],explain:"C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub> has two fewer hydrogens than the saturated acyclic C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>14</sub>, so it must contain either one C=C double bond or one ring."},
{ch:11,q:"For the C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub> compound in the previous scenario, no peak is observed at m/z = 69 (a loss of 15 from the molecular ion). What does this indicate?",choices:["The molecule has no &minus;CH<sub>3</sub> group to lose, ruling out methylcyclopentane and branched or terminal-alkene structures","The molecule must contain exactly one &minus;CH<sub>3</sub> group","The molecular ion itself must be unstable and undetectable","The compound must be acyclic with a terminal double bond"],explain:"No peak at m/z = 69 (84 &minus; 15) shows the molecule has no CH<sub>3</sub> group to lose, ruling out methylcyclopentane and branched or terminal-alkene structures, consistent with cyclohexane."},
{ch:11,q:"Why does a covalent bond's vibration frequency depend on the masses of the bonded atoms and the bond's strength?",choices:["A covalent bond behaves somewhat like a spring, and both a stiffer (stronger, often multiple) bond and lighter atoms result in higher vibration frequency","Vibration frequency depends only on the overall molar mass of the whole molecule","Vibration frequency depends only on the polarity of the bond, not on atomic mass or bond strength","All covalent bonds vibrate at exactly the same frequency regardless of the atoms involved"],explain:"A covalent bond behaves somewhat like a spring: lighter atoms and stronger (multiple) bonds vibrate at higher frequency."},
{ch:11,q:"What determines whether a bond or vibration is IR active?",choices:["The vibration must produce a change in the molecule's dipole moment","The bond must be a single bond rather than a double or triple bond","The two bonded atoms must have identical electronegativity","The molecule must contain at least one chiral carbon"],explain:"A bond or vibration only absorbs IR radiation if it produces a change in the molecule's dipole moment as it vibrates."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is the N&equiv;N bond in N<sub>2</sub> IR inactive?",choices:["Stretching a bond between two identical atoms causes no change in dipole moment","Triple bonds are always IR inactive, regardless of the atoms involved","Nitrogen atoms cannot vibrate within a molecule","N<sub>2</sub> absorbs IR radiation too strongly to be detected"],explain:"A bond between two identical atoms, such as N&equiv;N in N<sub>2</sub>, is IR inactive, since stretching it causes no change in dipole."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is CO<sub>2</sub> an effective greenhouse gas despite its symmetric stretch being IR inactive?",choices:["Its asymmetric stretch and bending modes both create a temporary net dipole and so are IR active","All of CO<sub>2</sub>'s vibrational modes, including the symmetric stretch, are IR active","CO<sub>2</sub> absorbs visible light rather than infrared radiation","CO<sub>2</sub> has no vibrational modes at all, but still absorbs IR radiation"],explain:"CO<sub>2</sub>'s asymmetric stretch and bending modes create a temporary net dipole and are IR active, making it an effective greenhouse gas despite its inactive symmetric stretch."},
{ch:11,q:"In which wavenumber range does a C=O bond typically absorb IR radiation?",choices:["1700&ndash;1750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","3200&ndash;3600 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1050&ndash;1410 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2850&ndash;3090 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"A C=O bond gives a strong, sharp absorption at 1700&ndash;1750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:11,q:"In which wavenumber range does an O&minus;H bond in a simple alcohol typically absorb, and how does this compare with the O&minus;H of a carboxylic acid?",choices:["3200&ndash;3600 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, a narrower and higher-wavenumber band than the broad 2500&ndash;3000 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> band of a carboxylic acid O&minus;H","2500&ndash;3000 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, identical to the carboxylic acid O&minus;H band","1700&ndash;1750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, the same range as a C=O bond","3300&ndash;3500 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, identical to an N&minus;H bond"],explain:"An alcohol O&minus;H absorbs at 3200&ndash;3600 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, narrower and at higher wavenumber than the broad 2500&ndash;3000 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> carboxylic acid O&minus;H band."},
{ch:11,q:"Why does the O&minus;H of a carboxylic acid give a broader, lower-wavenumber IR absorption than the O&minus;H of a simple alcohol?",choices:["Hydrogen bonding, often involving dimerization, is especially strong in carboxylic acids, which broadens and lowers the frequency of the O&minus;H absorption","Carboxylic acids contain no hydrogen bonding at all, unlike alcohols","The O&minus;H bond in a carboxylic acid is a double bond, unlike in an alcohol","Carboxylic acids absorb at a higher wavenumber than alcohols because their O&minus;H bond is stronger"],explain:"Hydrogen bonding broadens and lowers the frequency of an O&minus;H absorption, and is very strong (often dimerized) in carboxylic acids."},
{ch:11,q:"A sharp, strong IR absorption at 1700&ndash;1750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> is a reliable signature of which bond, and why can it not alone distinguish between several classes of compound?",choices:["A C=O bond; this band appears in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, esters and amides alike, so other evidence (such as a broad O&minus;H band) is needed to distinguish between them","A C&equiv;C bond; this band is unique to alkynes and distinguishes them from all other classes immediately","An O&minus;H bond; this band appears only in alcohols and nowhere else","A C&minus;H bond; this band appears in every organic compound equally and gives no useful structural information"],explain:"A band at 1700&ndash;1750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> signals a C=O bond, present in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, esters and amides alike, so other evidence is needed to distinguish between these classes."},
{ch:11,q:"Compounds C and D both have molecular formula C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. Compound C shows a broad absorption around 2950 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, plus bands at 1715 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> and 1250 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Compound D shows bands only at 1738 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> and 1240 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, with no broad absorption near 2950 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What are C and D?",choices:["C is propanoic acid (shows the broad carboxylic acid O&minus;H band); D is an ester such as methyl ethanoate or ethyl methanoate (no O&minus;H band)","C is an ester; D is propanoic acid","Both C and D must be propanoic acid, since they share the same molecular formula","C is propan-1-ol; D is propan-2-ol"],explain:"The broad band near 2950 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, together with the C=O and C&minus;O bands, identifies C as propanoic acid; D, lacking the broad O&minus;H band, must be an ester of the same formula."},
{ch:11,q:"In which wavenumber range does a C&equiv;C bond typically absorb IR radiation?",choices:["2100&ndash;2260 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1620&ndash;1680 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","3300&ndash;3500 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1050&ndash;1410 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"A C&equiv;C bond absorbs at 2100&ndash;2260 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, with variable intensity."},
{ch:11,q:"What property must an atomic nucleus have to be detected by NMR spectroscopy, and which nuclei commonly show it?",choices:["An odd number of nucleons, giving nuclear spin; examples include <sup>1</sup>H, <sup>13</sup>C, <sup>19</sup>F and <sup>31</sup>P","An even number of nucleons, giving zero nuclear spin; examples include <sup>12</sup>C and <sup>16</sup>O","A complete outer electron shell; examples include noble gas nuclei","A permanent magnetic dipole moment found only in transition metal nuclei"],explain:"Nuclei with an odd number of nucleons, including <sup>1</sup>H, <sup>13</sup>C, <sup>19</sup>F and <sup>31</sup>P, possess nuclear spin and are detectable by NMR."},
{ch:11,q:"What are equivalent protons, and what do they produce in a <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum?",choices:["Hydrogen nuclei in identical chemical environments, related by the molecule's symmetry, which give rise to a single, shared signal","Any two hydrogen atoms bonded to the same carbon, which always give two separate signals","Hydrogen nuclei bonded to different carbons, which always give a single combined signal regardless of environment","Protons that have lost their electron entirely, forming H<sup>+</sup> ions detectable by NMR"],explain:"Equivalent protons, in identical chemical environments related by symmetry, give rise to a single shared signal in the <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum."},
{ch:11,q:"How many <sup>1</sup>H NMR signals does methane, CH<sub>4</sub>, show, and why?",choices:["1 signal, since all four hydrogen atoms are equivalent by symmetry","4 signals, one for each hydrogen atom","2 signals, since methane has two different types of bond","0 signals, since methane contains no distinct proton environments"],explain:"Methane shows 1 signal, since all four hydrogen atoms are equivalent, each bonded to a carbon attached to three other identical H atoms."},
{ch:11,q:"How many <sup>1</sup>H NMR signals does propan-1-ol, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH, show?",choices:["4 signals: one for each of the three distinct carbons' hydrogens, plus one for the OH proton","3 signals, since the OH proton does not produce a separate signal","8 signals, one for every hydrogen atom in the molecule","2 signals, since propan-1-ol is a symmetrical molecule"],explain:"Propan-1-ol is not symmetrical, so each of its three carbons' hydrogens is a distinct environment, plus the OH proton is a fourth environment: 4 signals."},
{ch:11,q:"How many <sup>1</sup>H NMR signals does butane, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, show?",choices:["2 signals: one from the two equivalent CH<sub>3</sub> groups, one from the two equivalent CH<sub>2</sub> groups","4 signals, one for each carbon's hydrogens","1 signal, since butane is a fully symmetrical molecule","10 signals, one for every hydrogen atom in the molecule"],explain:"Butane is symmetrical, giving two CH<sub>3</sub> environments (equivalent to each other) and two CH<sub>2</sub> environments (equivalent to each other): 2 signals."},
{ch:11,q:"What does chemical shift, &delta;, measure in a <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum, and in what units?",choices:["The position of a signal along the horizontal axis, measured in parts per million (ppm)","The total number of hydrogen atoms producing a signal, measured in moles","The strength of the external magnetic field, measured in tesla","The number of neighbouring protons, measured as a dimensionless integer"],explain:"Chemical shift, &delta;, is the position of a signal along the horizontal axis, measured in parts per million (ppm)."},
{ch:11,q:"What does it mean for a proton to be deshielded, and how does this affect its chemical shift?",choices:["Electron density is pulled away from it by a nearby electronegative atom or group, exposing it more to the external field and increasing its chemical shift","Electron density is pushed towards it, exposing it less to the external field and decreasing its chemical shift","It becomes surrounded by extra electrons, which has no effect on chemical shift at all","It loses its nuclear spin entirely, removing it from the spectrum"],explain:"A deshielded proton has electron density pulled away by a nearby electronegative atom or group, exposing it more to the external field and resonating at a higher chemical shift."},
{ch:11,q:"According to typical chemical shift data, in which range does the aromatic Ar&minus;H proton of a benzene ring resonate?",choices:["6.9&ndash;9.0 ppm","0.9&ndash;1.0 ppm","3.3&ndash;3.7 ppm","9.4&ndash;10.0 ppm"],explain:"An aromatic Ar&minus;H proton typically resonates at 6.9&ndash;9.0 ppm."},
{ch:11,q:"According to typical chemical shift data, in which range does the aldehyde &minus;CHO proton resonate?",choices:["9.4&ndash;10.0 ppm","2.0&ndash;2.5 ppm","4.5&ndash;6.0 ppm","0.9&ndash;1.0 ppm"],explain:"An aldehyde &minus;CHO proton typically resonates at 9.4&ndash;10.0 ppm, among the most deshielded C&minus;H environments."},
{ch:11,q:"Why are the chemical shifts of &minus;OH and &minus;NH protons unusually variable, compared with C&minus;H protons?",choices:["Hydrogen bonding, which depends strongly on concentration, solvent and temperature, shifts their signal considerably","&minus;OH and &minus;NH protons have no nuclear spin, so their position cannot be measured precisely","&minus;OH and &minus;NH protons are always found at exactly 0 ppm, regardless of conditions","&minus;OH and &minus;NH protons do not actually appear in a <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum at all"],explain:"Hydrogen bonding, which depends strongly on concentration, solvent and temperature, shifts &minus;OH and &minus;NH signals considerably, making their position unusually variable."},
{ch:11,q:"What does the area under a signal (shown by the integrated trace) represent in a <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum?",choices:["A quantity directly proportional to the number of hydrogen nuclei producing that signal","The exact chemical shift value of that signal, in ppm","The number of neighbouring protons responsible for splitting that signal","The strength of the magnetic field applied to the sample"],explain:"The area under each signal is directly proportional to the number of hydrogen nuclei producing that signal."},
{ch:11,q:"Ethanal, CH<sub>3</sub>CHO, shows two <sup>1</sup>H NMR signals with integrated areas in the ratio 1:3. What does this ratio correspond to?",choices:["One CHO proton to three CH<sub>3</sub> protons","Three CHO protons to one CH<sub>3</sub> proton","One carbon atom to three oxygen atoms","One molecule of ethanal to three molecules of solvent"],explain:"The integrated areas of 1:3 directly reflect one CHO proton to three CH<sub>3</sub> protons in ethanal."},
{ch:11,q:"What causes a <sup>1</sup>H NMR signal to split into a cluster of closely spaced lines (a multiplet)?",choices:["Spin-spin coupling: the small magnetic field of a proton on a neighbouring carbon slightly modifies the effective field experienced by the observed proton(s)","Each hydrogen atom in the molecule always produces its own separate line, with no coupling involved","Differences in isotope mass among hydrogen atoms in the same environment","The presence of a chiral carbon elsewhere in the molecule"],explain:"Spin-spin coupling, in which the small magnetic field of a proton on a neighbouring carbon modifies the effective field of the observed proton(s), causes splitting into a multiplet."},
{ch:11,q:"What does the n + 1 rule state?",choices:["If a signal's protons have n equivalent protons on an immediately adjacent carbon, the signal is split into (n + 1) lines","If a signal's protons have n equivalent protons on an immediately adjacent carbon, the signal is split into n lines","The number of lines in a multiplet always equals the total number of hydrogen atoms in the molecule","The number of lines in a multiplet equals n&sup2; + 1, where n is the number of neighbouring protons"],explain:"The n + 1 rule: a signal is split into (n + 1) lines when there are n equivalent protons on an immediately adjacent carbon."},
{ch:11,q:"A signal is split into a quartet with relative intensities 1:3:3:1. How many equivalent protons are on the adjacent carbon?",choices:["3","4","2","1"],explain:"A quartet has 4 lines, so n + 1 = 4 and n = 3 equivalent protons on the adjacent carbon, with intensities following Pascal's triangle, 1:3:3:1."},
{ch:11,q:"Why do protons bonded to the same carbon not split each other's signal?",choices:["They are equivalent to each other and behave as a single group for the purposes of spin-spin coupling","Protons on the same carbon are always too far apart to interact magnetically","Protons on the same carbon carry opposite electrical charges and repel each other","Splitting only ever occurs between protons on the same carbon, never between different carbons"],explain:"Protons bonded to the same atom are equivalent to each other and do not split one another's signal, since they behave as a single group."},
{ch:11,q:"How do &minus;OH and &minus;NH protons typically behave with respect to spin-spin coupling, and what does this mean for their appearance?",choices:["They typically do not engage in coupling with neighbouring C&minus;H protons, so an &minus;OH or &minus;NH signal is almost always a singlet, and is not counted as a neighbour for other protons' splitting","They always couple strongly with every neighbouring C&minus;H proton, producing complex multiplets","They are always counted as neighbours when applying the n + 1 rule to adjacent C&minus;H protons","They never appear in a <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum, so the question of coupling does not arise"],explain:"&minus;OH (and &minus;NH) protons typically do not engage in spin-spin coupling with neighbouring C&minus;H protons; their own signal is almost always a singlet and they are not counted as a neighbour for other protons."},
{ch:11,q:"In ethanal, CH<sub>3</sub>CHO, what splitting pattern does the CHO proton's signal show, and why?",choices:["A quartet, since it has three neighbouring H atoms on the adjacent CH<sub>3</sub> carbon (n + 1 = 3 + 1 = 4)","A doublet, since it has one neighbouring H atom","A singlet, since the CHO proton has no neighbouring protons","A triplet, since it has two neighbouring H atoms"],explain:"The CHO proton has three neighbouring H atoms (the CH<sub>3</sub> group), so by the n + 1 rule it is split into 3 + 1 = 4 lines, a quartet."},
{ch:11,q:"In propanoic acid, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH, what splitting pattern does the CH<sub>2</sub> signal show, and why?",choices:["A quartet, since it neighbours the CH<sub>3</sub> group's three protons (the COOH proton, further away and non-coupling, does not count)","A triplet, since it neighbours the CH<sub>3</sub> group's three protons and the COOH proton together","A doublet, since it neighbours only the COOH proton","A singlet, since the CH<sub>2</sub> group has no neighbouring protons that couple"],explain:"The CH<sub>2</sub> protons neighbour the CH<sub>3</sub> group's 3 protons, giving n + 1 = 4, a quartet; the COOH proton does not count, being both further away and non-coupling."},
{ch:11,q:"In ethyl ethanoate, CH<sub>3</sub>COOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>, what splitting pattern would the terminal CH<sub>3</sub> of the ethyl group show, and why?",choices:["A triplet, since it neighbours the OCH<sub>2</sub> group's two protons (n + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3)","A quartet, since it neighbours the OCH<sub>2</sub> group's three protons","A singlet, since it has no neighbouring protons","A doublet, since it neighbours the acyl CH<sub>3</sub> group's protons across the ester link"],explain:"The terminal CH<sub>3</sub> of the ethyl group neighbours the OCH<sub>2</sub> group's 2 protons, giving n + 1 = 3, a triplet."},
{ch:11,q:"What additional structural information does a high-resolution <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectrum reveal that a low-resolution spectrum does not?",choices:["The fine splitting pattern of each signal, revealing the number of protons on each immediately adjacent carbon","The total number of proton environments in the molecule","The relative integrated area of each signal","The chemical shift of each signal, in ppm"],explain:"A high-resolution spectrum additionally resolves the fine splitting pattern of each signal, revealing the number of protons on each immediately adjacent carbon."},
{ch:11,q:"What does the shorthand '3H, t' used alongside an NMR signal indicate?",choices:["A signal from three equivalent protons, appearing as a triplet","A signal from three equivalent protons, appearing as a singlet","Three separate signals, each a different triplet","A signal at a chemical shift of exactly 3 ppm"],explain:"The shorthand records both integration and splitting together: '3H, t' means a three-proton signal appearing as a triplet."},
{ch:11,q:"What type of structural evidence does mass spectrometry primarily provide?",choices:["The relative molecular mass (from the molecular ion) and clues to specific groups and branching (from characteristic fragmentation)","The types of covalent bond present in the molecule","The number of distinct hydrogen environments in the molecule","The absolute configuration (R or S) at each chiral centre"],explain:"Mass spectrometry gives the relative molecular mass and, from characteristic fragmentation, clues to specific groups and branching present."},
{ch:11,q:"What type of structural evidence does infrared spectroscopy primarily provide?",choices:["The types of covalent bond, and therefore the functional groups, present in the molecule","The relative molecular mass of the compound","The number of neighbouring hydrogens on each carbon","The direction in which the compound rotates plane-polarized light"],explain:"Infrared spectroscopy identifies the types of covalent bond, and therefore the functional groups, present in a molecule."},
{ch:11,q:"What type of structural evidence does <sup>1</sup>H NMR spectroscopy primarily provide?",choices:["The number of distinct hydrogen environments, their relative numbers, their chemical surroundings, and the number of neighbouring hydrogens","The exact molecular formula of the compound, without any other technique","The boiling and melting points of the compound","The specific identity of any halogen atoms present"],explain:"Proton NMR reveals the number of distinct hydrogen environments, their relative numbers, their immediate chemical surroundings, and the number of neighbouring hydrogens."},
{ch:11,q:"Why is combining evidence from two or more analytical techniques usually necessary to confirm an unknown structure?",choices:["No single technique is normally conclusive alone; combined evidence provides much stronger, often definitive, support for one particular structure","Each technique only works for a specific, narrow range of molecular masses","Regulatory requirements demand that at least two techniques always be used","A single technique can only be used once per sample, due to sample destruction"],explain:"No single technique is normally conclusive alone; combining evidence from two or more techniques provides much stronger, often definitive, support for one particular structure."},
{ch:11,q:"Unknown X (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, M = 74) shows <sup>1</sup>H NMR with exactly two singlets in a 1:1 ratio, an IR spectrum with no broad O&minus;H band, and mass spectrum peaks at m/z = 74, 59 and 15. What is X?",choices:["Methyl ethanoate, CH<sub>3</sub>COOCH<sub>3</sub>","Propanoic acid, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH","Ethyl methanoate, HCOOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>","Propan-1,3-diol"],explain:"Only methyl ethanoate gives exactly two singlets in a 1:1 ratio with no broad O&minus;H band; the mass spectrum fragments (loss of 15, &minus;CH<sub>3</sub>, giving m/z 59, and CH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> at m/z 15) are consistent."},
{ch:11,q:"What is the recommended first step in a general strategy for deducing an unknown structure from combined analytical data, when elemental composition data is available?",choices:["Use the elemental composition to find the empirical formula","Immediately assign the splitting pattern of every <sup>1</sup>H NMR signal","Measure the optical rotation of the compound in a polarimeter","Assume the compound is a straight-chain alkane until proven otherwise"],explain:"The general strategy starts with using elemental composition (if given) to find the empirical formula, then uses the mass spectrum to find the molecular formula, then IR for functional groups, then NMR for the carbon skeleton."}
]
